---
import Image from '~/components/common/Image.astro';
import DshArticleLayout from '~/layouts/DshArticleLayout.astro';
import settingsImage from '~/assets/images/dsh/remote-access-settings.png';
import runningImage from '~/assets/images/dsh/mobile-harness-running.png';

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'HowTo',
  name: 'How to use DeepSeek Harness from an Android phone',
  description: 'Install DSH Mobile Remote, pair it from DeepSeek Harness settings, and open the normal DSH Web UI.',
  totalTime: 'PT10M',
  supply: [
    { '@type': 'HowToSupply', name: 'Android phone running Android 8.0 or newer' },
    { '@type': 'HowToSupply', name: 'Computer running DeepSeek Harness 0.1.0-rc.6' },
  ],
  step: [
    {
      '@type': 'HowToStep',
      name: 'Install DSH Mobile and the DSH plugin',
      text: 'Install the signed Android APK and the pinned GitHub plugin, then start DSH Web.',
      url: 'https://dshmobile.online/download/',
    },
    {
      '@type': 'HowToStep',
      name: 'Create a pairing code',
      text: 'Open Settings > Remote Access in DSH and create a six-digit pairing code or QR code.',
    },
    {
      '@type': 'HowToStep',
      name: 'Pair the phone',
      text: 'Sign in on the mobile app, scan the QR code or enter the six-digit code, then select the online computer.',
    },
    {
      '@type': 'HowToStep',
      name: 'Run a normal Harness task',
      text: 'Create a task, submit instructions, and follow its progress in the normal DSH Web interface.',
    },
  ],
};
---

<DshArticleLayout
  title="How DSH Mobile Remote works"
  description="Pair an Android phone with a computer running DeepSeek Harness, then open the real DSH Web UI through an outbound-only Relay connection."
  icon="tabler:route"
  structuredData={schema}
>
  <h2>The connection path</h2>
  <p>
    DeepSeek Harness continues to listen on <code>127.0.0.1:3080</code>. The Companion plugin opens an outbound WSS
    connection to the configured Relay. The phone authenticates with its account, asks for a short-lived WebView ticket,
    and opens the forwarded DSH interface.
  </p>

  <div class="info-grid not-prose">
    <section>
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold">What stays local</h3>
      <p class="mt-3 leading-7 text-muted">
        The DSH listener and device credential stay on the computer. No inbound public port is created.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold">What crosses the Relay</h3>
      <p class="mt-3 leading-7 text-muted">
        Authenticated HTTP and WebSocket traffic needed to render and operate the DSH Web UI.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>

  <h2>Create and manage the pairing</h2>
  <p>
    Open <strong>Settings &gt; Remote Access</strong> in DSH. You can create a QR code or six-digit code, view recent phone
    access, and remove the pairing from the same screen.
  </p>
  <Image
    src={settingsImage}
    alt="Remote Access settings inside DeepSeek Harness"
    width={800}
    height={747}
    class="w-full"
  />

  <h2>Use the real Harness interface</h2>
  <p>
    The remote WebView keeps the normal workspace, mode, model, task composer, conversation, injected context, reasoning
    status, elapsed time, and stop control. It is not a separate task API or a simplified replica.
  </p>
  <Image
    src={runningImage}
    alt="A DeepSeek Harness task running in the mobile remote WebView"
    width={430}
    height={932}
    class="mx-auto max-h-[760px] w-auto"
  />

  <h2>When the computer is unavailable</h2>
  <p>
    The phone shows the computer as offline when the computer or DSH is stopped, or when the plugin is disconnected from
    the Relay. The Companion follows the DSH Web process and does not need a separate background process.
  </p>

  <p>
    Ready to try it? <a href="/download/">Download the Android app</a>. For a controlled network boundary, follow the
    <a href="/self-hosted-relay/">private Relay guide</a>.
  </p>
</DshArticleLayout>
